# Solution

We solve $u_t = k\,u_{xx}$ on $0 < x < L$, $t>0$, with
$$u_x(0,t)=0,\qquad u_x(L,t)=-h\,u(L,t),\qquad u(x,0)=f(x).$$

## Separation of variables

Set $u(x,t)=X(x)T(t)$. Then $T'/(kT)=X''/X=-\lambda$, giving
$$T(t)=e^{-\lambda k t},\qquad X''+\lambda X=0,$$
with boundary conditions
$$X'(0)=0,\qquad X'(L)=-h\,X(L). \tag{BC}$$

This is a regular Sturm–Liouville problem (self-adjoint), so eigenfunctions for distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal on $[0,L]$ with weight $1$.

### $\lambda=0$: $X=A+Bx$, $X'(0)=B=0$, so $X=A$; then $X'(L)=0=-hA$ forces $A=0$ since $h\neq 0$. **No zero eigenvalue.**

### $\lambda<0$, write $\lambda=-\mu^2$ ($\mu>0$): $X=A\cosh(\mu x)+B\sinh(\mu x)$. $X'(0)=B\mu=0\Rightarrow B=0$, so $X=A\cosh(\mu x)$. The Robin condition gives
$$\mu\sinh(\mu L)=-h\cosh(\mu L)\;\Longrightarrow\;\mu\tanh(\mu L)=-h. \tag{E$-$}$$
Since $\mu\tanh(\mu L)>0$ for $\mu>0$, this requires $h<0$. The function $\mu\mapsto \mu\tanh(\mu L)$ is strictly increasing from $0$ to $+\infty$, so **exactly one negative eigenvalue exists when $h<0$**, and none when $h>0$.

### $\lambda>0$, write $\lambda=\beta^2$ ($\beta>0$): $X=A\cos(\beta x)+B\sin(\beta x)$. $X'(0)=B\beta=0\Rightarrow B=0$, so $X=A\cos(\beta x)$. The Robin condition gives
$$-\beta\sin(\beta L)=-h\cos(\beta L)\;\Longrightarrow\;\boxed{\;\beta\tan(\beta L)=h\;}. \tag{E$+$}$$

**When $h>0$**: $\tan(\beta L)>0$, so one root $\beta_n$ lies in each interval $\beta L\in\bigl(n\pi,\;n\pi+\tfrac{\pi}{2}\bigr)$, $n=0,1,2,\dots$ Infinitely many positive eigenvalues $\beta_n^2$.

**When $h<0$**: $\tan(\beta L)<0$, so one root $\beta_n$ lies in each interval $\beta L\in\bigl(n\pi+\tfrac{\pi}{2},\;(n+1)\pi\bigr)$, $n=0,1,2,\dots$ Infinitely many positive eigenvalues, **plus** the single negative eigenvalue $-\mu_0^2$ from (E$-$).

## Norms (orthogonality weights)

For a positive eigenvalue $\beta_n$ with eigenfunction $\cos(\beta_n x)$:
$$\int_0^L\cos^2(\beta_n x)\,dx=\frac{L}{2}+\frac{\sin(2\beta_n L)}{4\beta_n}.$$
Using $\tan(\beta_n L)=h/\beta_n$ and $\sin(2\beta_n L)=2\sin(\beta_n L)\cos(\beta_n L)=2\cos^2(\beta_n L)\tan(\beta_n L)$ with $\cos^2(\beta_n L)=\beta_n^2/(\beta_n^2+h^2)$:
$$\boxed{\;N_n:=\int_0^L\cos^2(\beta_n x)\,dx=\frac{L(\beta_n^2+h^2)+h}{2(\beta_n^2+h^2)}\;}. \tag{N$+$}$$

For the negative eigenvalue $-\mu_0^2$ ($h<0$) with eigenfunction $\cosh(\mu_0 x)$:
$$\int_0^L\cosh^2(\mu_0 x)\,dx=\frac{L}{2}+\frac{\sinh(2\mu_0 L)}{4\mu_0}=\frac{L}{2}+\frac{\sinh(\mu_0 L)\cosh(\mu_0 L)}{2\mu_0}.$$
Using $\tanh(\mu_0 L)=-h/\mu_0=|h|/\mu_0$ and $\cosh^2(\mu_0 L)=\mu_0^2/(\mu_0^2-h^2)$:
$$\sinh(\mu_0 L)\cosh(\mu_0 L)=\frac{|h|\,\mu_0}{\mu_0^2-h^2},$$
so
$$\boxed{\;N_0:=\int_0^L\cosh^2(\mu_0 x)\,dx=\frac{L(\mu_0^2-h^2)-h}{2(\mu_0^2-h^2)}\;}. \tag{N$-$}$$

---

## (a) Solution for $h>0$

All eigenvalues are positive: $\{\beta_n^2\}_{n=0}^{\infty}$, where $\beta_n$ are the positive roots of
$$\beta\tan(\beta L)=h,\qquad \beta_n L\in\Bigl(n\pi,\;n\pi+\frac{\pi}{2}\Bigr).$$
The eigenfunctions $\{\cos(\beta_n x)\}$ form a complete orthogonal set on $[0,L]$. Expanding $f(x)$ and using orthogonality with weights (N$+$):

$$\boxed{\;u(x,t)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}A_n\cos(\beta_n x)\,e^{-k\beta_n^2 t},\qquad A_n=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^L f(x)\cos(\beta_n x)\,dx}{\,N_n\,}\;}$$

with $N_n$ given by (N$+$). Since every $\beta_n^2>0$, every mode decays and $u(x,t)\to 0$ as $t\to\infty$ (consistent with heat escaping through the Robin end $x=L$ when $h>0$).

---

## (b) Solution for $h<0$

There is **one negative eigenvalue** $-\mu_0^2<0$, where $\mu_0>0$ is the unique root of
$$\mu\tanh(\mu L)=-h\;(=|h|),$$
with eigenfunction $\cosh(\mu_0 x)$; and a sequence of positive eigenvalues $\{\beta_n^2\}_{n=0}^{\infty}$, where $\beta_n$ are the positive roots of
$$\beta\tan(\beta L)=h,\qquad \beta_n L\in\Bigl(n\pi+\frac{\pi}{2},\;(n+1)\pi\Bigr),$$
with eigenfunctions $\cos(\beta_n x)$. The full set $\{\cosh(\mu_0 x),\,\cos(\beta_0 x),\,\cos(\beta_1 x),\dots\}$ is complete and orthogonal on $[0,L]$. Expanding $f$:

$$\boxed{\;u(x,t)=A_0\cosh(\mu_0 x)\,e^{k\mu_0^2 t}+\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}A_n\cos(\beta_n x)\,e^{-k\beta_n^2 t}\;}$$

where
$$A_0=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^L f(x)\cosh(\mu_0 x)\,dx}{\,N_0\,},\qquad A_n=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^L f(x)\cos(\beta_n x)\,dx}{\,N_n\,},$$
with $N_0$ from (N$-$) and $N_n$ from (N$+$).

**Physical interpretation.** When $h<0$ the boundary condition $u_x(L,t)=-h\,u(L,t)=|h|\,u(L,t)$ pumps heat *into* the rod whenever $u(L,t)>0$. The negative eigenvalue produces the **growing mode** $e^{k\mu_0^2 t}$: unless $f$ is exactly orthogonal to $\cosh(\mu_0 x)$ (i.e. $A_0=0$), the solution grows exponentially and there is no steady state. The remaining $\cos(\beta_n x)$ modes still decay, but they are dominated by the single unstable $\cosh$ mode as $t\to\infty$.

---

### Summary of the two cases

| | $h>0$ | $h<0$ |
|---|---|---|
| Eigenvalue eqn (positive $\lambda=\beta^2$) | $\beta\tan(\beta L)=h$, $\beta L\in(n\pi,n\pi+\pi/2)$ | $\beta\tan(\beta L)=h$, $\beta L\in(n\pi+\pi/2,(n+1)\pi)$ |
| Negative eigenvalue | none | one: $-\mu_0^2$, $\mu\tanh(\mu L)=-h$ |
| Eigenfunctions | $\cos(\beta_n x)$ | $\cosh(\mu_0 x)$ and $\cos(\beta_n x)$ |
| Long-time behavior | $u\to 0$ (decay) | $u\sim A_0\cosh(\mu_0 x)e^{k\mu_0^2 t}$ (growth, unless $A_0=0$) |

